  "query_preview": "..Visible satellite imagery today shows cumulus\ndeveloping over the higher terrain areas of norther Arizona, which\nwill likely lead to some isolated showers and possibly a\nthunderstorm or two in areas with locally higher dewpoints.\nSimilar conditions are expected for Friday as a ridge of high \npressure tries to build into the area. The ridge will be weak and\ndoesn't really get established as a trough starts to develop over\nthe Pacific Northwest and digs southward.\n\nStarting Saturday, there will be a shift in the weather across \nthe Desert Southwest as the Pacific Northwest trough starts to dig\nto the south. As the trough digs south, it will interact with \nwhat will become the remnants of TS Rachel. Model runs continue to\ndrag that moisture up into the Desert Southwest over the weekend \nand into early next week. The trough looks to become cut off by \nlate in the weekend and models are slow to push the low off to the\nnorth and east. With the potential for a prolonged period of \ntropical moisture being pulled northward into the region through, \nit looks like we could have a very active and wet period ahead of \nus. QPF ranges are fairly widespread at this time but the \npotential for 1-3\" with locally higher amounts remain possible. \nThis could lead to flood/flash flooding concerns, but exact\nimpacts will depend if most of the rain falls as heavy showers and\nthunderstorms with high rain rates with a lot of rain in a short\nperiod of time, or if this is more of a stratiform rain event \nwith light to moderate rainfall over a couple of days, with some \nembedded areas of heavier rainfall. The former would support more \nof a flash flooding threat while the latter would lead to high \nwater in streams and rivers affecting low water crossings or poor\ndrainage areas, leading to ponding of water on roads.\n\nCurrent projections have western areas seeing the greatest \npotential for heavy rain around the Sunday into Monday time period\nbut any slight shifts in the track could push things to the east.\nAs Rachel weakens and the trough starts to dig south, we should \nstart to see a better picture of just where the greatest impacts \nwill be expected. On top of the potential for heavy rainfall, we \nwill see increasing south to southwest winds with gusts 30-45 mph\non Sunday. Keep any eye on the forecast as we get more refined \nover the next day or two.",
